1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,460
Imagine waking up tomorrow and
discovering that the police in

2
00:00:03,690 --> 00:00:06,840
your neighbourhood no longer
take orders from the government.

3
00:00:07,605 --> 00:00:09,625
They take orders from someone else.

4
00:00:10,245 --> 00:00:11,845
The government still exists.

5
00:00:12,005 --> 00:00:15,385
The laws still exist.
The courts still exist.

6
00:00:15,865 --> 00:00:17,365
But there is a new question.

7
00:00:17,805 --> 00:00:19,365
Who is actually in charge?

8
00:00:20,747 --> 00:00:26,207
We all know what a state looks like.
It has borders. A flag. A government.

9
00:00:26,287 --> 00:00:30,087
A constitution. But what
actually makes it a state?

10
00:00:31,147 --> 00:00:34,627
The sociologist Max Weber
had a famous answer.

11
00:00:35,547 --> 00:00:39,607
He described the modern state as a
human community that successfully claims

12
00:00:39,837 --> 00:00:44,267
the monopoly of the legitimate use of
physical force within a given territory.

13
00:00:44,984 --> 00:00:47,084
That sounds extremely academic.

14
00:00:47,504 --> 00:00:50,644
But the basic idea is surprisingly simple.

15
00:00:51,084 --> 00:00:54,264
A country can contain millions
of different organisations.

16
00:00:55,388 --> 00:00:58,208
Companies. Clubs. Religious organisations.

17
00:00:58,708 --> 00:01:01,908
Private security companies.
Even armed groups.

18
00:01:02,390 --> 00:01:04,890
But the state says: "When
it comes to the ultimate

19
00:01:05,120 --> 00:01:07,890
use of force, that
authority belongs to me."

20
00:01:08,217 --> 00:01:10,477
If your neighbour steals
your car, you don't

21
00:01:10,707 --> 00:01:12,697
get to create your own police department.

22
00:01:13,457 --> 00:01:16,857
If someone attacks you, you don't
get to build your own prison.

23
00:01:17,173 --> 00:01:20,193
And if two people disagree
over who is right, they aren't

24
00:01:20,423 --> 00:01:23,513
supposed to bring their own
armies and settle the argument.

25
00:01:24,093 --> 00:01:26,633
The state is supposed to handle that.

26
00:01:27,234 --> 00:01:29,634
Police. Courts. Prisons. The military.

27
00:01:30,381 --> 00:01:35,961
It's an enormous system built around one
basic function: making rules enforceable.

28
00:01:36,471 --> 00:01:39,951
By the way, if you like videos
like this, consider subscribing.

29
00:01:40,571 --> 00:01:42,171
It really helps the channel.

30
00:01:44,561 --> 00:01:46,641
Now comes the interesting part.

31
00:01:46,901 --> 00:01:52,121
A state can have a constitution. It can
have laws. It can have a parliament.

32
00:01:52,201 --> 00:01:54,701
It can have impressive
government buildings.

33
00:01:55,299 --> 00:01:59,619
But if it can't enforce its decisions,
something fundamental has changed.

34
00:02:00,139 --> 00:02:03,879
A rule that nobody can
enforce is still written down.

35
00:02:04,299 --> 00:02:06,539
It just no longer decides anything.

36
00:02:07,596 --> 00:02:12,776
Imagine a government passes a law saying:
"Starting tomorrow, stealing is illegal."

37
00:02:13,116 --> 00:02:15,216
But the police don't have enough people.

38
00:02:15,636 --> 00:02:19,216
The courts don't function.
The prisons are empty.

39
00:02:19,643 --> 00:02:24,283
And armed groups in different parts of
the country simply do whatever they want.

40
00:02:24,965 --> 00:02:26,525
The law still exists.

41
00:02:26,885 --> 00:02:28,805
The parliament still exists.

42
00:02:29,145 --> 00:02:30,985
The flag is still flying.

43
00:02:31,065 --> 00:02:34,005
But the state is gradually losing control.

44
00:02:34,753 --> 00:02:36,133
And now we have a problem.

45
00:02:36,213 --> 00:02:38,393
Who is actually in charge?

46
00:02:40,743 --> 00:02:42,683
Something like this can
happen when a central

47
00:02:42,913 --> 00:02:46,063
government becomes too weak
to control its territory.

48
00:02:46,923 --> 00:02:48,843
Other centres of power appear.

49
00:02:49,481 --> 00:02:50,761
Militias. Tribal groups.

50
00:02:50,991 --> 00:02:53,481
Warlords. Criminal organisations.

51
00:02:53,561 --> 00:02:55,161
Religious movements.

52
00:02:55,572 --> 00:02:57,652
And each one may have its own rules.

53
00:02:58,352 --> 00:03:01,272
In one part of a city, the
government's laws apply.

54
00:03:02,152 --> 00:03:05,652
A few streets away, an armed
group may impose its own.

55
00:03:05,789 --> 00:03:10,109
And if that group has enough people and
enough weapons, it can enforce them.

56
00:03:10,643 --> 00:03:14,283
Suddenly, you have something that looks
surprisingly similar to a state...

57
00:03:14,443 --> 00:03:16,223
without actually being one.

58
00:03:18,653 --> 00:03:21,273
Lebanon is an interesting
example, because the

59
00:03:21,503 --> 00:03:24,953
situation there is not
simply "there is no state."

60
00:03:25,639 --> 00:03:27,519
There is a Lebanese government.

61
00:03:27,819 --> 00:03:29,519
There is a national army.

62
00:03:29,899 --> 00:03:31,599
There is a police force.

63
00:03:31,939 --> 00:03:35,679
But for decades, Hezbollah
has kept its own armed forces,

64
00:03:36,179 --> 00:03:39,139
alongside extensive political
and social structures.

65
00:03:39,886 --> 00:03:43,686
This has led to Hezbollah being
described as "a state within a state."

66
00:03:44,398 --> 00:03:48,958
In March 2026, that stopped
being a theoretical problem.

67
00:03:49,038 --> 00:03:50,718
Hezbollah fired on Israel.

68
00:03:51,458 --> 00:03:55,258
The same day, the Lebanese government
banned its military activities.

69
00:03:55,903 --> 00:03:58,063
"The decision of war and
peace," said the prime

70
00:03:58,293 --> 00:04:01,163
minister, "rests
exclusively with the state."

71
00:04:01,983 --> 00:04:03,963
The war went on for weeks anyway.

72
00:04:05,063 --> 00:04:08,243
So if the government has the
legal authority to use force...

73
00:04:08,323 --> 00:04:10,943
but another organisation
can start a war from

74
00:04:11,173 --> 00:04:13,523
its territory without the
government's consent...

75
00:04:13,683 --> 00:04:15,663
who actually has the monopoly?

76
00:04:18,603 --> 00:04:23,203
And then there is an even more
extreme example. The Islamic State.

77
00:04:23,727 --> 00:04:26,887
When ISIS controlled large
parts of Iraq and Syria,

78
00:04:26,967 --> 00:04:29,867
it wasn't simply a gang
hiding weapons somewhere.

79
00:04:30,586 --> 00:04:33,606
It had an army. Police. Courts.
Taxation. Administration.

80
00:04:33,836 --> 00:04:35,246
Its own rules.

81
00:04:35,509 --> 00:04:39,249
And, most importantly, the
ability to enforce those rules.

82
00:04:39,999 --> 00:04:45,419
That did not make ISIS an internationally
recognised state. It wasn't.

83
00:04:45,721 --> 00:04:48,981
But for a period of time, in
the territory it controlled, it

84
00:04:49,211 --> 00:04:52,461
performed many functions
normally associated with a state.

85
00:04:52,856 --> 00:04:54,896
And that reveals something important.

86
00:04:55,656 --> 00:04:57,756
A state isn't just a piece of paper.

87
00:04:58,396 --> 00:05:01,136
It also has to actually
control its territory.

88
00:05:04,236 --> 00:05:06,736
Now imagine something even stranger.

89
00:05:07,416 --> 00:05:09,636
What if the government
disappeared tomorrow?

90
00:05:10,435 --> 00:05:14,195
The people wouldn't disappear.
Neither would the buildings.

91
00:05:14,655 --> 00:05:16,135
The roads would still be there.

92
00:05:16,215 --> 00:05:17,735
The money would still exist.

93
00:05:18,135 --> 00:05:21,695
And on the first day, perhaps
almost nothing would look different.

94
00:05:22,391 --> 00:05:25,091
People would wake up. Go
to work. Buy groceries.

95
00:05:25,171 --> 00:05:27,391
Send their children to school.

96
00:05:27,815 --> 00:05:30,015
And then someone would ask: "Wait...

97
00:05:30,095 --> 00:05:31,975
who is arresting people today?"

98
00:05:32,225 --> 00:05:36,025
Because that's the moment when you
discover what a state actually is.

99
00:05:36,631 --> 00:05:41,211
It isn't just the president. It isn't
just parliament. It isn't the flag.

100
00:05:41,955 --> 00:05:44,035
It's an enormous network
of institutions and

101
00:05:44,265 --> 00:05:47,355
people capable of saying:
"This is the rule."

102
00:05:47,703 --> 00:05:50,143
And when someone answers: "I don't care."

103
00:05:50,703 --> 00:05:54,303
there has to be someone capable of
saying: "Then you're coming with me."

104
00:05:56,913 --> 00:05:59,093
And that creates a strange paradox.

105
00:05:59,893 --> 00:06:04,873
The more successful a state is, the less
we actually notice its monopoly on force.

106
00:06:05,545 --> 00:06:08,805
Most of the time, nobody is
forcing us to do anything.

107
00:06:09,405 --> 00:06:11,325
We follow the rules voluntarily.

108
00:06:12,171 --> 00:06:14,951
We pay our taxes. We stop at red lights.

109
00:06:15,551 --> 00:06:17,931
We don't simply take
other people's property.

110
00:06:18,333 --> 00:06:22,819
Not because there is a police
officer standing behind every person.

111
00:06:22,899 --> 00:06:26,019
But because everyone knows that
if we cross certain lines...

112
00:06:26,199 --> 00:06:27,619
the state can intervene.

113
00:06:28,171 --> 00:06:31,951
And that's why a successful state
can appear to use very little force.

114
00:06:32,751 --> 00:06:36,931
Its ability to use force is part of
the reason it usually doesn't have to.

115
00:06:39,971 --> 00:06:41,991
So... what makes a state a state?

116
00:06:42,477 --> 00:06:43,677
A flag?

117
00:06:43,921 --> 00:06:45,261
A constitution?

118
00:06:45,783 --> 00:06:46,983
Borders?

119
00:06:47,266 --> 00:06:48,466
Elections?

120
00:06:49,147 --> 00:06:50,347
Perhaps.

121
00:06:51,181 --> 00:06:53,601
But if a state can no
longer enforce its decisions

122
00:06:53,831 --> 00:06:56,501
across the territory
it claims to control...

123
00:06:56,821 --> 00:06:58,701
something else begins to emerge.

124
00:06:59,511 --> 00:07:03,891
Parallel power. Militias.
Warlords. Criminal organisations.

125
00:07:04,071 --> 00:07:05,291
Competing authorities.

126
00:07:05,887 --> 00:07:08,947
And eventually, perhaps,
an entirely new state.

127
00:07:09,809 --> 00:07:12,589
Because history keeps showing
us something uncomfortable.

128
00:07:13,729 --> 00:07:17,109
When two different powers claim
authority over the same territory,

129
00:07:17,329 --> 00:07:20,929
the crucial question is not simply
who has the legal right to rule.

130
00:07:21,113 --> 00:07:23,433
It's: who can actually enforce the rules?

131
00:07:26,143 --> 00:07:28,083
So let's go back to the beginning.

132
00:07:28,683 --> 00:07:31,663
The police in your neighbourhood
take orders from someone else.

133
00:07:32,183 --> 00:07:33,423
Who is in charge?

134
00:07:34,464 --> 00:07:36,844
The obvious answer is: the government.

135
00:07:37,544 --> 00:07:39,724
But the deeper answer
is: whoever has enough

136
00:07:39,954 --> 00:07:42,584
power to enforce its authority over you.

137
00:07:42,853 --> 00:07:44,653
And perhaps that is one
of the most fundamental

138
00:07:44,883 --> 00:07:47,453
questions we can ask about any state.

139
00:07:48,293 --> 00:07:49,853
Who has the final say?

140
00:07:50,281 --> 00:07:53,501
Inside a country, the state
is supposed to have it.

141
00:07:54,041 --> 00:07:56,901
But who has the final say
over the states themselves?

142
00:07:58,110 --> 00:08:00,610
We looked at that
question in another video.

143
00:08:01,110 --> 00:08:02,440
You'll find it right here.
